<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Majeerteen</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Majerteen</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Majeerteen are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalis">Somali</a> clan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darod">Darod</a> family, living mainly in the north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalia">Somalia</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puntland">Puntland</a>. In the 18th and 19th centuries their leaders ruled the Majeerteen Sultanate and the Sultanate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hobyo">Hobyo</a>, which accepted Italian protection and were conquered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in the 1920s. The clan has remained politically prominent, and Puntland was founded largely on its territory.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/majeerteen.jpg" alt="Majeerteen" width="320" height="303" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vincenzo Filonardi · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ahmed_Tahir-_Governor_of_Bosaso.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Majeerteen, also known as Mohammed Harti, belong to the Harti branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darod">Darod</a>. They traditionally inhabit the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bari">Bari</a>, Nugaal and Mudug regions, from Bosaso to Garacad, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-aden">Gulf of Aden</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a>, and are most numerous in the regional capitals of Bosaso, Garowe and Galkacyo. Migrations that began in the 19th century took some to Kismayo in the south, and some live in the Dollo Zone of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somali-region">Somali Region</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>. Historically the clan formed part of a coastal trading network around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> and the Indian Ocean.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first recorded mention of the clan in English is by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-salt">Henry Salt</a>, who described them as living on the coast of Hafun under a Sultan Hassan. Salt relates that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">Imam</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscat">Muscat</a> had sent gifts and asked leave to build a small fort there, and that both the request and the gifts were refused. In the 19th century Majeerteen merchants were active across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a>. One of them, Ahmed Muhammad, was granted the island of Baka by the Damoheita Afar, and his family ruled it as sultans into the period of Italian rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a>.</p><p>The Majeerteen Sultanate was founded in the 15th or 16th century and rose to prominence in the 19th under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boqor-osman-mahamuud">Boqor Osman Mahamuud</a>. It was nearly destroyed by his struggle with his cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yusuf-ali-kenadid">Yusuf Ali Kenadid</a>, who was forced into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>, returned with a band of Hadhrami musketeers, overpowered the local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawiye">Hawiye</a> clans and founded the Sultanate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hobyo">Hobyo</a> in 1878.</p><p>Kenadid signed a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> treaty with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, and Boqor Osman followed in late 1889. Each hoped to use Italian support against the other and to avoid direct occupation. When Kenadid refused to let British troops land in his territory to fight the Dervish forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammed-abdullah-hassan">Mohammed Abdullah Hassan</a>, the Italians exiled him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a> and then to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a>. After two years of resistance by Boqor Osman and Majeerteen rebels, which Italian forces put down with long coastal bombardments of towns and naval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockades</a>, the territory came under the full authority of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> by late 1927.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osman-yusuf-kenadid">Osman Yusuf Kenadid</a>, a son of the first sultan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hobyo">Hobyo</a>, devised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osmanya">Osmanya script</a> for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalis">Somali</a> language in the 1920s. Members of the clan held many government posts in the 1960s and 1970s, among them Abdirashid Shermarke, the first prime minister and second president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalia">Somalia</a>. In the 1980s and 1990s the Somali Salvation Democratic Front was a predominantly Majeerteen political group.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Majeerteen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The sultanate took shape in the mid-18th century among the <b>Majeerteen</b> clan of the Darod in the far north-east of Somalia.</blockquote><a href="/state/majeerteen-sultanate">Majeerteen Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
